<h1>fill</h1>
<hr align="center" size="5" class="whs3">
<p class="whs4"><span lang="EN-US"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">This command is accessible from</font></span> <i style="font-style:normal;"><span class="Path" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Mesh &gt; Fill</b></span></i> <span lang="EN-US">or by pressing</span> <span class="Shortcut" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Ctrl + F</b></span><span lang="EN-US">.</span></p>
<p class="Description"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial; font-weight:bold;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;"><b>Description</b></font></span><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">:</font></span> <span class="Command">Fill</span> <span lang="EN-US">command reconstructs missing data (holes) in selected areas of the mesh.&#160;</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-US">This command is grayed if there is no selection or all selections are hidden. It works on wholly selected entities as well as on partial selections. It is recommended to use <u><a href="Stitch.htm">Stitch</a></u> command in case of long thin holes like cracks or splits.</span></p>
<p class="Note" id="P1"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold; font-style:normal;"><b>Note</b></span><span lang="EN-US">:</span> <span lang="EN-US" style="font-style:normal;">Filling holes on meshes with faulty areas will lead to visible defects on the result mesh surface. It is recommended to use functions</span> <span class="Command">Remove Faulty Areas</span> <span lang="EN-US" style="font-style:normal;">and</span> <span class="Command">Reduce Noise</span> <span lang="EN-US" style="font-style:normal;">before performing</span> <span class="Command">Fill</span> <span lang="EN-US" style="font-style:normal;">operations.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;"><img src="../mesh/Fill_holes_files/image002.gif" width="257px" height="165px" border="0" class="img_whs5" alt=""></font></span></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><b style="font-weight:bold;"><span lang="EN-US">Curvature based filling</span></b></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">Works well on both flat and curved areas. Best results are obtained on curved surfaces. This option is switched on by default.</font></span></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><b style="font-weight:bold;"><span lang="EN-US">Flat filling</span></b></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">This method works well on flat surfaces only. It is faster than the curvature based filling, but the resulting mesh structure could appear irregular in some cases.</font></span></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><b style="font-weight:bold;"><span lang="EN-US">Fill small holes only</span></b></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">This option is used to restrict the number of filled holes by the number of their boundary edges. The default value is 50 edges.</font></span></p>
<p class="Warning"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">Important: It is recommended to use the curvature based fill option on partially selected mesh. If the whole mesh is selected it is possible to attempt filling the boundary mesh contour and the results could be unexpected.</font></span></p>
<p class="Use"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Work around</b></span><span lang="EN-US">: If the whole mesh is selected and the Curvature based fill is chosen, check the option Fill small holes only to avoid processing the mesh largest boundary.</span></p>
